  "notes": "Measured on the 787-10 HZ-AR29 (left, 2025-01-03). The cheatline's colors were sampled in sun; the bands keep their heights from nose to tail and dip into the nose. The white leading-edge strip runs the whole fin. Part of the fleet still wore the 2012 scheme (ivory, navy fin with the gold palm and swords) in 2025 (e.g. the NEOM-titled 787-10, Commons 2025-07-19); the freighters still wear it (SVA/cargo). Which is commonest in late 2026 isn't counted: 'commonest' set here as the scheme being rolled out fleet-wide, a doubt. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on HZ-AR29 (787-10, left, 2025-01-03): saudia runs x 0.181-0.282 (length 0.101, from 0.085), the Arabic 0.35-0.436 (0.086); the wordmark's lowercase is 0.85 of its tallest letter (the d). Kept on the 787-10 (drawnOn B78X); Saudia's commonest type is the A320neo family, where the titles and the cheatline's nose dip will sit differently as shares: a re-measure on an A320neo photo is left to do. The cheatline bands are level straight runs with a dip at the nose.",
  "history": "New file (Saudia had no scheme here). Unveiled 2023-09-30, a revival of the 1970s 'Saudia' colors, replacing the 2012 ivory-and-navy scheme with the gold emblem."
}
